  "worked_solution": "According to the reduction of order method, we let ${y}_{2}=u(t){t}^{2}.$\n\nDifferentiating the above with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{\u2032} & ={u}^{\u2032}{t}^{2}+2ut \\\\ {y}_{2}^{\u2033} & ={u}^{\u2033}{t}^{2}+4{u}^{\u2032}t+2u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2}({u}^{\u2033}{t}^{2}+4{u}^{\u2032}t+2u)-2t({u}^{\u2032}{t}^{2}+2ut)+2(u{t}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ {u}^{\u2033}{t}^{4}+4{u}^{\u2032}{t}^{3}+2u{t}^{2}-2{u}^{\u2032}{t}^{3}-4u{t}^{2}+2u{t}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {u}^{\u2033}{t}^{4}+2{u}^{\u2032}{t}^{3} & =0 \\\\ {t}^{3}(t{u}^{\u2033}+2{u}^{\u2032}) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${t}^{3}\\ne 0$ for $t>0,$ we must have\n\n$$\nt{u}^{\u2033}+2{u}^{\u2032}=0.\n$$\n\nLet $w={u}^{\u2032}.$ Then our equation becomes\n\n$$\nt{w}^{\u2032}+2w=0.\n$$\n\nThis is a separable equation that has the solution\n\n$$\nw=\\frac{{c}_{1}}{{t}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nBut since $w={u}^{\u2032},$ we have\n\n$$\n{u}^{\u2032}=\\frac{{c}_{1}}{{t}^{2}}\\,\u27f9\\,u=-\\frac{{c}_{1}}{t}+{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our expression for ${y}_{2}(t)=u{t}^{2}$ becomes\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(t)=(-\\frac{{c}_{1}}{t}+{c}_{2}){t}^{2}.\n$$\n\nSetting ${c}_{1}=-1$ and ${c}_{2}=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(t)=t.\n$$\n\nFinally, because $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=-{t}^{2}\\ne 0,$ we know that ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are independent (you should verify this calculation yourself).\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A{t}^{2}+Bt.\n$$",
